{"assessment":"The source provides the statistical mean-field effective detuning diagnostic delta_eff = delta - (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4) in the main text (lines 573-575) and End Matter (lines 707-710), with the interface energy scale eta(delta) = (1/4)*sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4) (lines 697-699). The routing hint specifies public control delta with discrete values {1,2,3} and a benchmark mechanism parameter d_H_fixed=1.5, with private branches c=1 (statistical-interface correction) and c=0 (homogeneous single-mode baseline). The source supports the formula with c=1 (the (4-2*d_H) factor) and the homogeneous baseline c=0 corresponds to the single-mode mean-field with no interface correction (delta_eff = delta). The source does not explicitly state the domain delta in [1,3] for this diagnostic; the hint provides this as the intended public control. The source states the diagnostic is a necessary-condition lower bound for the critical detuning, not the true numerical critical point. The extraction includes one mechanism with the effective detuning equation, one control (delta), one readout (delta_eff), and two claims for the two branches. The case is authoring-ready because the source supports a numeric control-to-readout relation with explicit parameters.","case_authoring_ready":true,"claims":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":576,"line_start":573,"support":"The equation for delta_eff is given, and it is stated that this provides a lower bound for the critical detuning."},{"line_end":710,"line_start":707,"support":"The same equation is given in the End Matter, with the statement that bistability is a necessary condition."}],"applicability":"This diagnostic is a necessary condition for the phase transition; it provides a lower bound for the critical detuning. It is not the true numerical critical point or an exact experimental measurement.","claim_id":"statistical_interface_effective_detuning","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[1.0,2.0,3.0],"anchors":[{"line_end":570,"line_start":568,"support":"The paper discusses detuning values Delta = 1.0 gamma, 2.0 gamma, and 3.0 gamma."}],"control_name":"delta","domain_kind":"numeric_values","domain_statement":"The paper explicitly discusses detuning values Delta = 1.0 gamma, 2.0 gamma, and 3.0 gamma in the context of the lower critical dimension.","maximum":null,"minimum":null}],"control_names":["delta"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["statistical_interface_correction"],"mechanism_ids":["statistical_mean_field_effective_detuning"],"oracle_outputs":{"delta_eff":{"args":[{"var":"delta"},{"args":[{"param":"c_interface"},{"args":[{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"four_minus_2dH_coefficient"},{"args":[{"param":"two_coefficient"},{"param":"d_H_fixed"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"subtract"},{"param":"four_coefficient"}],"op":"divide"},{"args":[{"args":[{"args":[{"var":"delta"},{"param":"two_coefficient"}],"op":"power"},{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"gamma_rate"},{"param":"two_coefficient"}],"op":"power"},{"param":"four_coefficient"}],"op":"divide"}],"op":"add"},{"param":"half_coefficient"}],"op":"power"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"subtract"}},"quantitative_form":"delta_eff = delta - c*(4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4)","readout_names":["delta_eff"],"statement":"For a fractal driving pattern with statistical-interface correction (c=1), the effective detuning is delta_eff = delta - (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4). This is a necessary-condition lower-bound diagnostic for the critical detuning, not the true numerical critical point.","statistical_basis":"Analytical mean-field derivation, no statistical aggregation.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"},{"anchors":[{"line_end":623,"line_start":613,"support":"The single-mode mean-field equation for homogeneous 2D driving has no interface term, so delta_eff = delta."}],"applicability":"This is the homogeneous single-mode baseline without interface corrections, used for comparison with the statistical-interface case.","claim_id":"homogeneous_single_mode_effective_detuning","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[1.0,2.0,3.0],"anchors":[{"line_end":570,"line_start":568,"support":"The paper discusses detuning values Delta = 1.0 gamma, 2.0 gamma, and 3.0 gamma."}],"control_name":"delta","domain_kind":"numeric_values","domain_statement":"The paper explicitly discusses detuning values Delta = 1.0 gamma, 2.0 gamma, and 3.0 gamma in the context of the lower critical dimension.","maximum":null,"minimum":null}],"control_names":["delta"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["homogeneous_single_mode_baseline"],"mechanism_ids":["statistical_mean_field_effective_detuning"],"oracle_outputs":{"delta_eff":{"args":[{"var":"delta"},{"args":[{"param":"c_homogeneous"},{"args":[{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"four_minus_2dH_coefficient"},{"args":[{"param":"two_coefficient"},{"param":"d_H_fixed"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"subtract"},{"param":"four_coefficient"}],"op":"divide"},{"args":[{"args":[{"args":[{"var":"delta"},{"param":"two_coefficient"}],"op":"power"},{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"gamma_rate"},{"param":"two_coefficient"}],"op":"power"},{"param":"four_coefficient"}],"op":"divide"}],"op":"add"},{"param":"half_coefficient"}],"op":"power"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"subtract"}},"quantitative_form":"delta_eff = delta - c*(4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4)","readout_names":["delta_eff"],"statement":"For a homogeneous single-mode baseline (c=0), the effective detuning is delta_eff = delta, i.e., no interface correction. This corresponds to the standard single-mode mean-field description.","statistical_basis":"Analytical mean-field derivation, no statistical aggregation.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"}],"controls":[{"allowed_values":[1.0,2.0,3.0],"anchors":[{"line_end":570,"line_start":568,"support":"The paper discusses detuning values Delta = 1.0 gamma, 2.0 gamma, and 3.0 gamma."}],"definition":"Detuning between the driving frequency and the frequency of the spatially uniform, lowest-frequency bosonic mode, in units of gamma.","domain_kind":"numeric_values","domain_statement":"The paper explicitly discusses detuning values Delta = 1.0 gamma, 2.0 gamma, and 3.0 gamma in the context of the lower critical dimension.","maximum":null,"minimum":null,"name":"delta","unit":"gamma"}],"fixed_conditions":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The formula includes the (4-2*d_H) factor."}],"condition_id":"statistical_interface_correction","statement":"The statistical mean-field effective detuning includes the interface correction term (4-2*d_H)*eta(delta), corresponding to c=1 in the diagnostic."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":623,"line_start":613,"support":"The single-mode mean-field equation for homogeneous 2D driving has no interface term, so delta_eff = delta."}],"condition_id":"homogeneous_single_mode_baseline","statement":"The homogeneous single-mode baseline corresponds to no interface correction, i.e., c=0, giving delta_eff = delta."}],"mechanisms":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":576,"line_start":572,"support":"The statistical mean-field theory yields an effective detuning that displays bistability when delta_eff > sqrt(3)/2 gamma, providing a lower bound for the critical detuning."},{"line_end":710,"line_start":707,"support":"The equation of state exhibits bistability when delta_eff = delta - (4-2*d_H)*eta(delta) > sqrt(3)/2 gamma."}],"equations":["delta_eff = delta - (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4)","eta(delta) = (1/4) * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4)"],"mechanism_id":"statistical_mean_field_effective_detuning","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The equation includes gamma as the dissipation rate; the paper uses gamma as the unit for detuning values."}],"name":"gamma_rate","role":"Dissipation rate, used as the unit of detuning; set to 1 in the equation.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"gamma","value":1.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The inequality delta_eff > sqrt(3)/2 * gamma is stated."}],"name":"sqrt3_over_2_gamma","role":"Threshold for bistability: delta_eff must exceed this value.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"gamma","value":0.8660254037844386},{"anchors":[{"line_end":699,"line_start":697,"support":"eta(delta) = (1/4)*sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4) is given."}],"name":"eta_coefficient","role":"Coefficient in the interface energy scale eta(delta) = (1/4)*sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4).","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.25},{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The formula delta_eff = delta - (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(...) includes the factor (4-2*d_H)."}],"name":"interface_correction_coefficient","role":"Coefficient multiplying (4-2*d_H) in the effective detuning formula; represents the statistical-interface correction.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":1.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The formula includes the constant 4 in (4-2*d_H)."}],"name":"four_minus_2dH_coefficient","role":"Constant 4 in the term (4-2*d_H).","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":4.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The formula includes the constant 2 in (4-2*d_H) and in delta^2."}],"name":"two_coefficient","role":"Constant 2 in the term (4-2*d_H) and in the exponent of delta^2.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":2.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The formula includes the constant 4 in the denominator and in gamma^2/4."}],"name":"four_coefficient","role":"Constant 4 in the denominator of the interface correction and in gamma^2/4.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":4.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The formula includes the square root, which is equivalent to an exponent of 1/2."}],"name":"half_coefficient","role":"Exponent 1/2 in the square root sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4).","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.5},{"anchors":[{"line_end":548,"line_start":548,"support":"The Sierpinski cross has d_H approximately 1.5."}],"name":"d_H_fixed","role":"Benchmark instantiation of the Hausdorff dimension, fixed at 1.5 as reported for the Sierpinski cross; used in the statistical mean-field diagnostic.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":1.5},{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The effective detuning formula includes the interface correction term (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(...), corresponding to c=1."}],"name":"c_interface","role":"Private branch parameter for the statistical-interface correction; multiplies the interface term (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4).","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":1.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":623,"line_start":613,"support":"The single-mode mean-field equation for homogeneous 2D driving has no interface term, so delta_eff = delta."}],"name":"c_homogeneous","role":"Private branch parameter for the homogeneous single-mode baseline; sets the interface correction to zero.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.0}],"statement":"Statistical mean-field theory incorporating kinetic energy contributions at interfaces between driven and undriven regions yields an effective detuning delta_eff = delta - (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4). Bistability, a necessary condition for the phase transition, occurs when delta_eff > sqrt(3)/2 * gamma. This provides a lower bound for the critical detuning.","variable_names":["delta_eff","delta","d_H","gamma","eta"]}],"paper_scope":"The paper investigates a driven-dissipative interacting bosonic system described by a non-equilibrium Gross-Pitaevskii equation, where the effective spatial dimension is controlled by the fractal shape of the driving field. It determines the lower critical fractal dimension for a first-order phase transition and shows it can be tuned via the detuning. The statistical mean-field effective detuning diagnostic provides a lower bound for the critical detuning as a function of the Hausdorff dimension.","readouts":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":575,"line_start":573,"support":"The definition of delta_eff is given in the equation."}],"definition":"Effective detuning in the statistical mean-field theory, defined as delta_eff = delta - (4-2*d_H)/4 * sqrt(delta^2 + gamma^2/4). It is a diagnostic for the necessary condition for bistability and phase transition.","name":"delta_eff","resolution":null,"statistic":"Deterministic formula, no statistical aggregation.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"gamma"}],"source":{"arxiv_versioned_id":"2506.20617v2","citation":"Mattheus Burkhard, Tunable Lower Critical Fractal Dimension for a Nonequilibrium Phase Transition, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/36fj-lsw7","journal_doi":"10.1103/36fj-lsw7"},"source_ambiguities":[]}
